will consider it.

[The remainder of this message is going to be a tangential minirant about
YANIs, and what posters can do to get them taken more seriously, at
least by me.  If you don't care, quit reading.]

First and foremost -- if you want me to think about it, then show me that
YOU'VE thought about it too -- and for longer than the thirty seconds it
took to write your post.  Explain in clear*, simple English what you
think the game should start doing, and why you think the game should
start doing it.  Include things like "what this will add to the game",
and "why the game needs this added".  Also include things like "what
keys will I press to have this show up"; the idea above offers two
different scenarios, one of which is relatively common (items in
inventory) and the other is rather uncommon (explicit far-look to a 
monster that an experienced player already knows; i.e. a yellow f
is a tiger).  The gameplay effect of both of these is noticeably
different, so this is actually two different ideas in one.

DO NOT simply post your mental image of what the output will look like;
I'm looking at you here, OP.  Those of us who _do_ write code, create
patches, and otherwise try to add to the game we all enjoy are NOT your 
personal code monkeys, and when you just randomly post output without 
any details, it looks like you're saying "Here's what I want, you figure 
out where it should go."  Fuck that; do it yourself if you want it so
badly.  The source is at http://www.nethack.org.

Second -- PLEASE do some forethought, and have some concept of how the
idea would impact the game.  There are three major branches right now:  
Vanilla, Slash'EM, and Sporkhack.  All three have different design 
philosophies, and only Slash'EM's is the "grab bag" type.  For any idea
to fit in either Vanilla or Spork, it is going to have to mesh well.
Things like "robot Terminator zombies with lightsabers" do not mesh 
well.  Of course, you'd know that if you had followed #1 above and
thought about it for more than thirty seconds.

Consider whether your idea will make other features in the game
obsolete.  "Add one, take one away" is only good when the thing you're
taking away wasn't being used in the first place (obscure polearms, 
anyone?).  Again, looking at the OP's post, it seems (we don't know for
sure, because he didn't post any details, just proposed output) that
low-INT characters would be subject to inability to determine what a 
particular monster was.  

Let's take a yellow f, a tiger, for example.

On-screen, it's a yellow f, which tells you just as much as you need to
know for a normal INT -- so we must now change yellow to grey, and
replace f with a generic character so the poor 4-INT Barbarian can only
know that this monster is an "animal".  We've taken two features away
now just to support the idea, and I can't imagine anyone having fun
trying to figure out whether they were fighting a rothe, a soldier ant,
a killer bee, or a housecat... just because they were INT 4 and wanted
to play a smashy Barbarian.

Third -- Please use correct terminology and be factually accurate about
the portions of the game you DO reference in your post.  Nethack doesn't
have IQ, it has INT.  An earlier post claimed that "a spellbook of charm
monster is identical to a scroll of charm monster"; a) it's a scroll of
taming, and b) the statement is obviously false, since the spell is
subject to failure chances and is reusable for 20,000 turns.

If you can't even manage to be factually accurate about the existing
game, it's very hard for me to take your idea about the future game 
seriously, because it just says that you aren't even really sure how the
game works right now.

Nethack is a long-lived game, and there are spoilers EVERYWHERE detailing 
almost every possible tidbit; it's quite possible to reference them or, I 
dunno, get more experience playing the game so that you didn't need to
reference them?  Your call, but the information is out there.

[rant concluded]

I do appreciate ideas, believe me.  I have no shortage of my own in the
queue already for Spork, but that doesn't mean I'm going to flat-out
refuse all other ideas simply because they weren't mine.  But by the
same token, getting pissy just because I don't automatically implement
every single idea that passes before my eyeballs is not going to improve 
your chances of me EVER implementing one of your ideas.

